[{:db/id 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-765f030b-c4cb-527c-8c26-c947b0ace6a4" "question-1cc7cc51-6281-53f8-b74f-55c2a0b2fa6e" "question-1e18756f-1e50-5f09-be71-26f001c4f68e" "question-0ae03bac-e2a8-5c3b-a5da-8c8f23590a03"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179645 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-07e888b6-1046-5ef1-87ef-0610d3939941" "question-71fbebcb-93ff-5be9-84c7-d1676b609367"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179483 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 8y\\,\\mathbf i + 4x\\,\\mathbf j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^2+y^2 = 4.$ If the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by  \n\n![](images/9d/9d24a5f692535453b1b34c2bc9236bd44cd2c88c05724a2a5dbdf804b98957e5.svg)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf n = \\dfrac x2\\,\\mathbf i + \\dfrac y2\\,\\mathbf j.$*" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179646 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e4ef9955-c544-5a0b-9748-af661e7750b3" "question-7f447fce-ea84-5549-9b80-d5d43d163662"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179647 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ab737fa4-bfd8-53e2-9695-4ad4cbdcb6c6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-765f030b-c4cb-527c-8c26-c947b0ace6a4" :question/id #uuid "765f030b-c4cb-527c-8c26-c947b0ace6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127028" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1d25942ae680c068b587f38bfbc968c8850b8fa2db2003e994e1ec32dad892.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7acc3028de66ae71ae4e425da639e51a357a77f2b0a345290592cac91ba17d3.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/04/04639a0074aa4607fa57bfe589aad1780f8da6f573d8589a68c2fdb569b5a129.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-406f40cf-3f8f-59a9-9607-1816ee0ec44b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id