ux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *in* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb4fd4068760bb9132052b26508f0e1e0c02c45d425770de804b17ce7c512919.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/46/46afd4cde01c95388c9daefbef0668ef696d5817ab9ea3614982edf58a9bdf55.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99e2f9e7-a7ef-5d8d-abe6-b747079ed849"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99e2f9e7-a7ef-5d8d-abe6-b747079ed849" :answer-field/id #uuid "99e2f9e7-a7ef-5d8d-abe6-b747079ed849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-05372253-4ce0-51d2-8c8a-6be80ef5e71b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd620493-1352-5d6a-ab86-993b4208f2b9" "answer-9073e798-9345-5c89-aa11-6f536f9781c5" "answer-5c350aee-ec57-55e6-825e-9ebcb6d46c5d" "answer-05372253-4ce0-51d2-8c8a-6be80ef5e71b" "answer-3efe06e4-d0d0-5a68-8a81-622c991444de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd620493-1352-5d6a-ab86-993b4208f2b9" :answer/id #uuid "dd620493-1352-5d6a-ab86-993b4208f2b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9073e798-9345-5c89-aa11-6f536f9781c5" :answer/id #uuid "9073e798-9345-5c89-aa11-6f536f9781c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c350aee-ec57-55e6-825e-9ebcb6d46c5d" :answer/id #uuid "5c350aee-ec57-55e6-825e-9ebcb6d46c5d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05372253-4ce0-51d2-8c8a-6be80ef5e71b" :answer/id #uuid "05372253-4ce0-51d2-8c8a-6be80ef5e71b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3efe06e4-d0d0-5a68-8a81-622c991444de" :answer/id #uuid "3efe06e4-d0d0-5a68-8a81-622c991444de" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1e18756f-1e50-5f09-be71-26f001c4f68e" :question/id #uuid "1e18756f-1e50-5f09-be71-26f001c4f68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127027" :question/problem "![](images/af/afaec113101f1a63134d2b1cb5c9744b93f9b84dabafaa205780d2dc5b49a7bc.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/6b/6b24ece279cc480f20d7a4dbc65f7a5257af71f22688b1416c73e3b40673a321.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/9e/9e8527d26f0f2bd5372d8643f8f9321ec022bb0079e1fc5b23be4978f436613c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9864b2a4-0034-5f3d-ba1e-cf4c021c366a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9864b2a4-0034-5f3d-ba1e-cf4c021c366a" :answer-field/id #uuid "9864b2a4-0034-5f3d-ba1e-cf4c021c366a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-29facd25-cb5e-5912-bb18-0cf1f7a74908" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5eb97c0d-6343-53ce-a7a9-c6e8df480d35" "answer-c0e131d5-1b27-5110-aeb8-3ce97e516ccd" "answer-29facd25-cb5e-5912-bb18-0cf1f7a74908" "answer-3a5a907e-06a3-50b5-82c0-14c80eee68f0" "answer-b1686369-3dee-56da-b3d5-fd5ec9dbeb68"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5eb97c0d-6343-53ce-a7a9-c6e8df480d35" :answer/id #uuid "5eb97c0d-6343-53ce-a7a9-c6e8df480d35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0e131d5-1b27-5110-aeb8-3ce97e516ccd" :answer/id #uuid "c0e131d5-1b27-5110-aeb8-3ce97e516ccd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29facd25-cb5e-5912-bb18-0cf1f7a74908" :answer/id #uuid "29facd25-cb5e-5912-bb18-0cf1f7a74908" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a5a907e-06a3-50b5-82c0-14c80eee68f0" :answer/id #uuid "3a5a907e-06a3-50b5-82c0-14c80eee68f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1686369-3dee-56da-b3d5-fd5ec9dbeb68" :answer/id #uuid "b1686369-3dee-56da-b3d5-fd5ec9dbeb68" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0ae03bac-e2a8-5c3b-a5da-8c8f23590a03" :question/id #uuid "0ae03bac-e2a8-5c3b-a5