tter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/56/56a7fc09ed2a2a15b0949fa6041dc9c0ee6beb69f30b1a5c048afd8c57887d52.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6033533a-9e89-5e03-a1f8-d82826d8190e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6033533a-9e89-5e03-a1f8-d82826d8190e" :answer-field/id #uuid "6033533a-9e89-5e03-a1f8-d82826d8190e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c78e6bb5-9afc-5b31-b499-d02078202d56" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4525744b-a33c-5d02-b83b-0cd4fd30e353" "answer-8da70b03-f65d-5594-a91e-19d3b3531590" "answer-05d52570-2557-595f-99e2-1b31c2d60dc8" "answer-e1eda114-26bd-5178-9bcc-2a69974022c6" "answer-c78e6bb5-9afc-5b31-b499-d02078202d56"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4525744b-a33c-5d02-b83b-0cd4fd30e353" :answer/id #uuid "4525744b-a33c-5d02-b83b-0cd4fd30e353" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8da70b03-f65d-5594-a91e-19d3b3531590" :answer/id #uuid "8da70b03-f65d-5594-a91e-19d3b3531590" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05d52570-2557-595f-99e2-1b31c2d60dc8" :answer/id #uuid "05d52570-2557-595f-99e2-1b31c2d60dc8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1eda114-26bd-5178-9bcc-2a69974022c6" :answer/id #uuid "e1eda114-26bd-5178-9bcc-2a69974022c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c78e6bb5-9afc-5b31-b499-d02078202d56" :answer/id #uuid "c78e6bb5-9afc-5b31-b499-d02078202d56" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180031 :question/problem "![](images/e6/e679efaa2612d45bbfe8b918de7148853b64eaf530af34a525ef1735e0946b09.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fb/fbcd6fd0fadcc955212a145de9edb4654ddb92ddebb8ba31eb775cff676efeeb.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/a3/a3d398d382e6bc94ddde77e650d50a6b5ec050083f89f64104f6f2fd172a9239.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177855 :answer-field/correct 17592186177859 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-07e888b6-1046-5ef1-87ef-0610d3939941" :question/id #uuid "07e888b6-1046-5ef1-87ef-0610d3939941" :question/math-academy-id "q-127058" :question/problem "![](images/65/653699a9c78577272425de7f8e6d015743e2c2e8c6e9a21bef189dc06fd80684.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{5}{y}^{7}\\,i+x{y}^{3}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](images/35/35e33731fbe85305a4308ef03c9f793b77558d66350d9345c30e643df1c40ba1.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{5}{y}^{7}\\,i+x{y}^{3}\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{5}{y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{5}{y}^{7}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e4055bff-eb04-5d8c-8a7f-fd7faf3e0c1d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e4055bff-eb04-5d8c-8a7f-fd7faf3e0c1d" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4055bff-eb04-5d8c-8a7f-fd7faf3e0c1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6e5de1a3-32b4-5e14-81b4-8c30c59f2613" :answer-field/choices ["answer-64bb62e3-bd57-57a6-9c6c-17776849a043" "answer-cf5e5cf8-0499-54ef-8365-3f3efa38105a" "answer-794f9e41-d152-5887-a25c-abf259a83c57" "answer-cf225ba4-1606-5bb5-954a-0c37ef35f638" "answer-6e5de1a3-32b4-5e14-81b4-8c30c59f2613"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64bb62e3-bd57-57a6-9c6c-17776849a043" :answer/id #uuid "64bb62e3-bd57-57a6-9c6c-17776849a043" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-x{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf5e5cf8-0499-54ef-8365-3f3efa38105a" :answer/id #uuid "cf5e5cf8-0499-54ef-8365-3f3efa38105a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-794f9e41-d152-5887-a25c-abf259a83c57" :answer/id #uuid "794f9e41-d152-5887-a25c-abf259a83c57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf225ba4-1606-5bb5-954a-0c37ef35f638" :answer/id #uuid "cf225ba4-1606-5bb5-954a-0c37ef35f638" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e5de1a3-32b4-5e14-81b4-8c30c59f2613" :answer/id #uuid "6e5de1a3-32b4-5e14-81b4-8c30c59f2613" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{5}{y}^{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-71fbebcb-93ff-5be9-84c7-d1676b609367" :question/id #uuid "71fbebcb-93ff-5be9-84c7-d1676b609367" :question/math-academy-id "q-127059" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b08e4cc9dd6ebde7f4bf544d7a76c4706dbd69a2c31d4c0fda8f816d4f871b5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+(x-y)^{2}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C$  shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{2}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=j.$ \n\n![](images/5d/5d0e9699f75e1f3f727c5c771fdce1a66311496cb55d31c0aac789901feed0cb.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}((x+y)^{2}\\,i+(x-y)^{2}\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x-y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(x-y)^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-544df328-c7fc-55b8-b971-da80f2afa474"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-544df328-c7fc-55b8-b971-da80f2afa474" :answer-field/id #uuid "544df328-c7fc-55b8-b971-da80f2afa474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1148ef69-d315-5d49-9d48-0b6ccac5465c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1148ef69-d315-5d49-9d48-0b6ccac5465c" "answer-fb0b8003-7828-51fb-91cc-f49acd49d338" "answer-02b163d2-8d88-51ac-abaa-42c491e74881" "answer-0caccbf9-4642-59fa-8f70-8c1bac70ef51" "answer-d961af8e-4079-56ce-98d9-0bbc2b4be0c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1148ef69-d315-5d49-9d48-0b6ccac5465c" :answer/id #uuid "1148ef69-d315-5d49-9d48-0b6ccac5465c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-y)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb0b8003-7828-51fb-91cc-f49acd49d338" :answer/id #uuid "fb0b8003-7828-51fb-91cc-f49acd49d338" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-(x-y)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02b163d2-8d88-51ac-abaa-42c491e74881" :answer/id #uuid "02b163d2-8d88-51ac-abaa-42c491e74881" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0caccbf9-4642-59fa-8f70-8c1bac70ef51" :answer/id #uuid "0caccbf9-4642-59fa-8f70-8c1bac70ef51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d961af8e-4079-56ce-98d9-0bbc2b4be0c9" :answer/id #uuid "d961af8e-4079-56ce-98d9-0bbc2b4be0c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e4ef9955-c544-5a0b-9748-af661e7750b3" :question/id #uuid "e4ef9955-c544-5a0b-9748-af661e7750b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-226115" :question/problem "![](images/57/575463a6fbe18d3f9b805e1a9c3d8e52bedd84a8db3a3c90d87381b8cb7abf74.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-4x\\,i-4y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/eb/ebe05a522f9f46acfed63e29927b46f41d78483b5df5e7a15a15ae5f33abd2f4.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-4x\\,i-4y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e89aabf0-d356-583a-a3cf-404273cddd70"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e89aabf0-d356-583a-a3cf-404273cddd70" :answer-field/id #uuid "e89aabf0-d356-583a-a3cf-404273cddd70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c4eec9b3-71ac-57f5-835f-95347213cc06" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1bbaa21-1588-571b-867f-6c07807e4cff" "answer-76a9acd9-336e-553e-ac03-9d29b38dee70" "answer-c4eec9b3-71ac-57f5-835f-95347213cc06" "answer-754059dd-7704-537c-8f5b-83a69628877d" "answer-d5afc158-099d-5fca-ba5b-ba5702d29c54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1bbaa21-1588-571b-867f-6c07807e4cff" :answer/id #uuid "b1bbaa21-1588-571b-867f-6c07807e4cff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76a9acd9-336e-553e-ac03-9d29b38dee70" :answer/id #uuid "76a9acd9-336e-553e-ac03-9d29b38dee70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4eec9b3-71ac-57f5-835f-95347213cc06" :answer/id #uuid "c4eec9b3-71ac-57f5-835f-95347213cc06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-754059dd-7704-537c-8f5b-83a69628877d" :answer/id #uuid "754059dd-7704-537c-8f5b-83a69628877d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5afc158-099d-5fca-ba5b-ba5702d29c54" :answer/id #uuid "d5afc158-099d-5fca-ba5b-ba5702d29c54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7f447fce-ea84-5549-9b80-d5d43d163662" :question/id #uuid "7f447fce-ea84-5549-9b80-d5d43d163662" :question/math-academy-id "q-226132" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f413fd04759cdb02118b320fea2f678712681a57f5b08e1bcb57a6843c0c80f0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=4x\\,i+4y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/87/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(4x\\,i+4y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =4{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12(3-(-3)) \\\\  & =12\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 72=288.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fd37f018-666a-5dbb-8a05-7813cb84c875"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fd37f018-666a-5dbb-8a05-7813cb84c875" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd37f018-666a-5dbb-8a05-7813cb84c875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d86896fc-5499-5228-a1a9-ce525916fc2c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dcc47f6e-dbd1-5cc9-912f-6e7a2036089f" "answer-7d25776a-11ff-529c-8f88-125b184a30fa" "answer-47a0ff6d-e708-565d-a280-3cadd01ffb31" "answer-d86896fc-5499-5228-a1a9-ce525916fc2c" "answer-f9651ba3-7ceb-59ea-8fd1-b5bacff9edfb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcc47f6e-dbd1-5cc9-912f-6e7a2036089f" :answer/id #uuid "dcc47f6e-dbd1-5cc9-912f-6e7a2036089f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "128" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d25776a-11ff-529c-8f88-125b184a30fa" :answer/id #uuid "7d25776a-11ff-529c-8f88-125b184a30fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "144" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47a0ff6d-e708-565d-a280-3cadd01ffb31" :answer/id #uuid "47a0ff6d-e708-565d-a280-3cadd01ffb31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "256" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d86896fc-5499-5228-a1a9-ce525916fc2c" :answer/id #uuid "d86896fc-5499-5228-a1a9-ce525916fc2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "288" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9651ba3-7ceb-59ea-8fd1-b5bacff9edfb" :answer/id #uuid "f9651ba3-7ceb-59ea-8fd1-b5bacff9edfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "360" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ab737fa4-bfd8-53e2-9695-4ad4cbdcb6c6" :question/id #uuid "ab737fa4-bfd8-53e2-9695-4ad4cbdcb6c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-226136" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9da6bdb9ab5f93d0b0d11dfd60ab2b647380a94ce337c2314ec3504cabafa889.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=4x\\,i-3y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(4x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(4x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-6.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-6)=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a01086e0-9b32-569e-ab08-30e8248e546d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a01086e0-9b32-569e-ab08-30e8248e546d" :answer-field/id #uuid "a01086e0-9b32-569e-ab08-30e8248e546d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-57aeb9a4-34ac-5fdf-99f7-f349f66e9ca1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10472c8b-0f2c-541f-a4f7-87022439fc30" "answer-83ba3fd3-59b5-593e-9d3a-53cc62cc9e50" "answer-57aeb9a4-34ac-5fdf-99f7-f349f66e9ca1" "answer-8fa05c49-f0b6-5d89-a641-050254ac8771" "answer-721de4ee-1215-587c-a230-6395ae1c468d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10472c8b-0f2c-541f-a4f7-87022439fc30" :answer/id #uuid "10472c8b-0f2c-541f-a4f7-87022439fc30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83ba3fd3-59b5-593e-9d3a-53cc62cc9e50" :answer/id #uuid "83ba3fd3-59b5-593e-9d3a-53cc62cc9e50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57aeb9a4-34ac-5fdf-99f7-f349f66e9ca1" :answer/id #uuid "57aeb9a4-34ac-5fdf-99f7-f349f66e9ca1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fa05c49-f0b6-5d89-a641-050254ac8771" :answer/id #uuid "8fa05c49-f0b6-5d89-a641-050254ac8771" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-721de4ee-1215-587c-a230-6395ae1c468d" :answer/id #uuid "721de4ee-1215-587c-a230-6395ae1c468d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180790 :tutorial/content "To calculate the flux of a vector field over a smooth, closed curve, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nIf $C=C_1\\cup C_2 \\cup \\cdots \\cup C_n$ is piecewise smooth, we have to calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over each smooth face. That is,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+ \\cdots +\n\\int\\limits_{C_n} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ on $C_i.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf i + (y-x)^3\\,\\mathbf j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown below. \n\n![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over $C,$ we sum the fluxes of $\\mathbf F$ over each side:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate how to compute these integrals, let's construct the flux integral across the side $C_4,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nThe side $C_4$ and its outward-pointing unit normal are shown below.\n\n![](images/06/06fd5a2ecbd709e8e835f2a57ef3ada772526366d51e5e16474d9b8635500e8e.png)\n\nSince this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = -\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\,i+(y-x)^{3}\\,j)\\cdot (-j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\,i)\\cdot (-j)+((y-x)^{3}\\,j))\\cdot (-j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}0-(y-x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(x-y)^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing a similar procedure for the other sides, we can construct the full expression for the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over $C.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179866 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
